\title{Amplitude Identifiability of a Knee Ladder\\[2mm]
\large Windows, quantisation, and the limits of a doubling sweep in data-free attention pruning}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{knee} of a pruned-attention quality curve: given a monotone curve $C$
recording the fraction of full-model held-out accuracy retained when each query attends
only to its top-$k$ scored keys, and a bar $b$, the knee is the least budget clearing the
bar. Knees are expensive, so they are measured by a coarse sweep --- here a grid of step
$s=32$ --- repeated at doubling context lengths $\ctx = 128\cdot 2^{i}$. A five-rung ladder
at depth $d=4$ produced, at a first random seed, knees $16,32,64,128,256$ matching the
product law $\kappa = d\,\ctx/32$ exactly at every rung; at a second seed it produced
$16,32,64,96,224$, a deficit of exactly one grid step at the two longest rungs, read as a
systematic sub-linear drift.

Our main tool is a \emph{Window Lemma}: a reported knee $k$ whose predecessor on the sweep
grid is $p$ places the continuous crossing point in $(p,k]$, using monotonicity alone.
Applied to the one-parameter multiplicative law $\kappa(i)=A\,2^{i}$, rung $i$ confines the
amplitude $A$ to $(p/2^{i},k/2^{i}]$, giving windows $(12,16]$ and $(14,16]$ at the first
seed and $(8,12]$ and $(12,14]$ at the second. Consequently: no amplitude explains both
second-seed rungs (their windows are disjoint), so the drift cannot be a re-fitted constant
of the mechanism; each broken rung alone is explainable, so the obstruction is a genuine
two-point conflict one measurement from resolution; the first-seed chain is sharply
identifiable, with the set of admissible amplitudes exactly $(14,16]$; and at
$\ctx = 4096$ the three surviving hypotheses predict reported knees in $[480,512]$,
$\le 384$ and $[416,448]$, pairwise separated by a full grid step. Every measured window
satisfies $8<A\le 16$, so the product-law budget remains a valid upper bound at both seeds
and all rungs, with a guaranteed $8\times$ and at most $16\times$ attention speedup.
We also show the knee functional is a left adjoint, that the reported grid knee is the
rounding of the continuous knee, and that a doubling ladder identifies the amplitude to
within $s/2^{N}$ after $N$ rungs and no better than $16/2^{N}$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measured object}

Attention over a context of length $\ctx$ costs $\Theta(\ctx^{2})$ per layer. \emph{Data-free
top-$k$ pruning} replaces the full attention pattern by one in which each query keeps only
its $k$ highest-scoring keys, selected without reference to calibration data. The relevant
empirical object is the \emph{retained-quality curve}
\[
  C(k) \;=\; \frac{\text{held-out accuracy of the top-}k\text{ model}}
                  {\text{held-out accuracy of the full model}},
\]
assumed monotone non-decreasing in $k$. Fixing a bar $b$ (here $b=0.98$), the \emph{knee} is
$k^{*} = \min\{k : C(k)\ge b\}$.

\begin{definition}[Grid knee]
For a finite grid $G\subset\mathbb{N}$, a bar $b$ and a curve $C$, say $k$ \emph{is the knee
of $C$ on $G$ at $b$} if $k\in G$, $b\le C(k)$, and $k\le j$ for every $j\in G$ with
$b\le C(j)$.
\end{definition}

The knee is unique when it exists, and every swept budget strictly below it fails the bar.

\subsection{The ladder and the product law}

At depth $d=4$ a five-rung ladder was run at $\ctx_i = 128\cdot 2^{i}$, $i=0,\dots,4$,
against the \emph{product law} $P_i = d\,\ctx_i/32 = 16\cdot 2^{i}$.

